WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.640
The A - Z of isms... orientalism.

00:00:03.680 --> 00:00:05.400
The late academic, Edward Said,

00:00:05.480 --> 00:00:07.240
developed the term orientalism

00:00:07.320 --> 00:00:10.200
to describe how Europeans
portray the orient

00:00:10.280 --> 00:00:14.080
as inferior, uncivilised
and all around weird.

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:17.720
But importantly, Said argued
this was also, simultaneously,

00:00:17.800 --> 00:00:20.160
about defining Europe
as the orient's opposite -

00:00:20.440 --> 00:00:23.800
superior, civilised
and all around wonderful.

00:00:23.880 --> 00:00:27.080
The actual term 'the orient' refers
to the rising of the sun in the east

00:00:27.160 --> 00:00:29.640
and comes from
the Latin word 'oriens'.

00:00:30.120 --> 00:00:32.400
So we can think of orientalism
as Eastern-ism

00:00:32.480 --> 00:00:34.240
or stereotyping of the East.

00:00:34.920 --> 00:00:38.720
Orientalism then, is the form
of knowledge, in quotation marks,

00:00:39.080 --> 00:00:42.360
that authorises and justifies
the assertion of Western power

00:00:42.440 --> 00:00:43.240
over the East.

00:00:43.840 --> 00:00:45.120
But what does this actually mean?

00:00:46.120 --> 00:00:49.680
Orientalism acts like a pair
of glasses that distort your vision

00:00:49.760 --> 00:00:52.200
and exaggerate cultural
and religious differences.

00:00:52.480 --> 00:00:53.560
With these glasses,

00:00:53.640 --> 00:00:56.720
just about everything and everyone
seems highly exotic and strange

00:00:56.800 --> 00:00:58.720
or worse, fanatical and dangerous.

00:00:59.320 --> 00:01:00.960
Women in the Middle East,
for example,

00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:03.320
appear only as exotic -
a belly dancer -

00:01:03.400 --> 00:01:06.280
or oppressed - a 'veiled woman' -

00:01:06.360 --> 00:01:08.080
with no nuances in between.

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:09.880
And in the early 20th Century,

00:01:09.960 --> 00:01:12.560
you might have seen men
portrayed as exotically romantic

00:01:12.640 --> 00:01:15.800
or more recently as crazy
and fanatical, a terrorist,

00:01:16.040 --> 00:01:18.480
and miss all the ordinary
human beings along the way.

00:01:19.480 --> 00:01:22.920
Many Arabs, Muslims and Asians
embrace this controversial term

00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:25.720
to describe the unique kind
of racism they experience.

00:01:26.120 --> 00:01:28.120
Orientalism encapsulates, for many,

00:01:28.200 --> 00:01:30.480
how their culture,
religion and ethnicity

00:01:30.560 --> 00:01:32.800
are so often reduced to a stereotype,

00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:36.920
causing their humanity to be
overlooked and culture misunderstood.

00:01:37.720 --> 00:01:39.840
Isn't this just a form
of stereotyping then?

00:01:40.160 --> 00:01:41.880
Well, yes and no.

00:01:42.480 --> 00:01:45.840
Orientalism is about a much
broader set of power relations.

00:01:46.160 --> 00:01:48.240
When the US government
views the Middle East

00:01:48.320 --> 00:01:49.800
through orientalist glasses

00:01:49.880 --> 00:01:52.880
it creates policies
with far-reaching consequences.

00:01:53.200 --> 00:01:55.720
From outright war waged
on other countries

00:01:55.800 --> 00:01:58.560
to imposing a travel ban
on whole groups of people.

00:01:59.160 --> 00:02:01.640
Perhaps the trickiest thing
about orientalism

00:02:01.720 --> 00:02:04.800
is that it's not fuelled by ignorance
or a simple lack of knowledge,

00:02:05.160 --> 00:02:06.160
just the opposite.

00:02:06.520 --> 00:02:09.160
It has historically
been produced as knowledge

00:02:09.240 --> 00:02:10.840
by those in positions of power,

00:02:10.920 --> 00:02:14.160
whether government officials,
religious figures, or the media.

00:02:14.600 --> 00:02:16.880
For example when
President Trump stated,

00:02:17.360 --> 00:02:18.640
"I think Islam hates us,

00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:20.760
we can't allow people
coming into this country

00:02:20.840 --> 00:02:22.800
who have this hated
for the United States."

00:02:23.240 --> 00:02:27.200
He effectively characterised
the 1.6 billion Muslims worldwide

00:02:27.520 --> 00:02:28.920
as anti-American.

00:02:29.520 --> 00:02:30.600
Even today,

00:02:30.680 --> 00:02:33.480
orientalism continues
because it is so ingrained

00:02:33.560 --> 00:02:37.080
in how we see other peoples
and cultures that it goes unnoticed.

00:02:37.480 --> 00:02:41.000
Even the term, 'The Middle East'
is considered to be orientalist,

00:02:41.080 --> 00:02:43.840
because it standardises
a Western viewpoint

00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:47.920
by referring to West Asia and North
Africa as the middle of the East.

00:02:48.560 --> 00:02:50.680
Much of what counts as knowledge
about the Middle East

00:02:50.760 --> 00:02:51.560
and parts of Asia

00:02:52.080 --> 00:02:55.440
is still produced through those
distorting and reductive glasses.

00:02:56.200 --> 00:02:58.840
And just like in the
18th and 19th Centuries,

00:02:59.160 --> 00:03:01.920
orientalism remains powerful
because it shapes,

00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:04.480
not just how people in the West
view the East,

00:03:04.800 --> 00:03:06.920
but also  how they view themselves.

00:03:10.160 --> 00:03:16.240
Thanks for watching. 
